The ground state of neutral nitrogen (N-7) has an electron configuration of [He] 2s² 2p³. The three electrons in the 2p subshell must be arranged to minimize energy. Using Hund's rules, the maximum spin multiplicity is achieved by having all three electrons in separate p orbitals with parallel spins, resulting in a total spin quantum number \( S = \frac{3}{2} \). This corresponds to the spin multiplicity \( 2S + 1 = 4 \), and since nitrogen's electron configuration requires three unpaired electrons, the total spin quantum number is \( \frac{3}{2} \). 

\boxed{C}

Answer: C

The charge of the object is –1 C.  
The elementary charge \( e \) is \( 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \) C per electron.  
To find the number of excess electrons \( n \):

\[
n = \frac{\text{Total charge}}{\text{Charge per electron}} = \frac{-1 \text{ C}}{-1.60 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C}}
= \frac{1}{1.60 \times 10^{-19}}
= 6.25 \times 10^{18}
\]

So, the object contains \( 6.25 \times 10^{18} \) excess electrons.

